# Lithium TIMECOP suppression file (variable-latency smoke).
#
# The mode-120 smoke (tools/timecop/run_sign_smoke.sh) gates ONLY on the
# KyberSlash variable-latency property: a secret (undefined) operand reaching a
# variable-latency instruction (div/mod/...). Secret-dependent CONTROL FLOW is
# out of scope here -- it is owned by the object-code constant-time scan
# (ct_scan_matrix.sh / tools/ct_scan.py) and the statistical dudect-sign
# harness. The sampler / approx_exp / IRS / packing code uses branchless
# constant-time idioms (full-table scans, masked selects, ct_lt/ct_sel/ct_is_zero)
# that GCC -O2 lowers to cmov/setcc; Memcheck reports each secret-conditioned
# cmov as "Conditional jump or move depends on uninitialised value". We suppress
# ONLY that error kind (Memcheck:Cond).
#
# We deliberately do NOT suppress Memcheck:Value: the patched Valgrind records a
# variable-latency hit as an Err_Value (same kind as a plain use-of-uninitialised
# value), so a blanket Value suppression would blind the gate. Residual
# "Use of uninitialised value" reports -- declassified signature/challenge output
# and branchless CT helpers -- are left visible, and the smoke greps for the
# "Variable-latency instruction operand" message so they cannot mask a real
# variable-latency leak.
{
   lithium-intentional-constant-time-cmov
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*
   ...
}
